{{also|crevasse}}

==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|lang=enm|crevice}}, from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|lang=fro|crevace}}, from {{term|lang=fro|crever||to break, burst}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|lang=la|crepare||to break, burst, crack}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkɹɛvɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[narrow]] [[crack]] or [[fissure]], in a [[rock]] or [[wall]].
#: ''I can't tell you how urbane and sprightly the old poll parrot was; and [...] not a pocket, not a '''crevice''', of pomp, humbug, respectability in him: he was fresh as a daisy.'' (William Butler Yeats)

